= Outlook

The system built in this thesis is a snapshot of a field that is still moving quickly, and its
lasting value may lie less in the exact numbers it reached than in the direction it points to.
Retrieval-augmented generation is maturing from a way of answering questions into the standard way
that organisations put their own knowledge in front of their people, and as that shift continues the
questions that mattered here will only grow in importance.

The clearest of these is trust. Most work still judges reliability after an answer is written, yet the
experience of this thesis suggests that the more durable place for it is before retrieval, attached to
the source itself. As corpora keep growing through self-service uploads and as more of their content
is produced with the help of language models, a document can no longer be assumed trustworthy simply
because it exists. A trust signal that travels with each source, computed automatically and refreshed
as the corpus changes, is likely to become a normal layer of enterprise retrieval rather than an
add-on. The limits found here point the way to a stronger version of it, one that can read agreement
and disagreement rather than mere co-mention, and that holds up on corpora beyond the single one it was
tuned on.

Retrieval itself is likely to keep widening its view. The pairing of vector search with a knowledge
graph proved its worth by catching what similarity search alone misses, and the natural next move is to
let the system reason over that structure more actively, deciding for itself when to search, when to
traverse and when it has gathered enough. As assistants take on longer and more open tasks, retrieval
becomes less a single lookup and more an ongoing conversation between the model and the knowledge base.

Two threads run underneath all of this. The first is evaluation. The harness built here made the
difference between the two system versions visible, and as these assistants move into daily use the
measuring will have to move with them, from offline benchmarks toward continuous, live assessment of
how real answers perform against real needs. The second is the human one. Every result in this work is
a metric, and metrics capture only part of what makes a learning assistant useful. Whether a learner
actually trusts the answer, acts on it, and comes back is a question that only people can answer, and
bringing them into the loop is the step that would turn a well-measured system into a genuinely
dependable one.

Seen this way, AVAILABLE 2.0 is less an endpoint than a foundation. It shows that fusing retrieval
paths and scoring sources before they are used makes an industrial assistant both more accurate and
more honest, and it leaves behind the tooling to keep testing that claim as the material, the models
and the expectations around it continue to change.
